Negative control — `default()` returns the ID, not the ROW (AC-5, ruling R5,
design D-4).

Mutates the collection context's `default:` member to expose `getDefault` — the
shared `useCollection` helper, which yields the whole mapped `Address` — in
place of the local `getDefaultId`, which yields `row.id`. Both are already in
scope in the same destructure, so the mutation is a one-token swap and the
member is still present, still callable, still truthy on a loaded list. Only
its TYPE changes, which is exactly the contract R5 fixed: ten in-tree consumer
expressions read `defaultAddress()?.id` before the conversion and each was
migrated to consume the id directly.

Declared AC — AC-5: "`default()` yields the default address's ID". The
read-back asserts `useContext().default() === <that row's id>` AND
`typeof … === "string"`; asserting truthiness does not discriminate between the
id and the row, which is precisely what this mutant exploits.

Collateral RED, measured, and NOT this control's declared subject:
  - AC-35 — the barrel's declared public surface (the member's shape is part of
    the enumerated contract)
  - AC-12 — the `setDefault` read-back, which feeds `default()`'s output back in
    as an id

Both are declared elsewhere. A red here that is accompanied by those two is the
expected signature of this one mutation, not three independent regressions.

